PUBLIC RESPONSE: F. None of the above

In rhyme-royal the Book of the Duchess is built out of seven-line stanzas A B A B B C C. All five of the forms you list actually occur line-finally in the poem and are used as rhyme partners.

* wente is the regular past of wende and is rhymed e.g. with sente / mente in the dream narration;
* here is a normal line-final rhyme word, rhymed with dere, bere etc. in the lament and the lover’s speeches;
* fool is rhymed in the usual way, and the same rhyme is the one that gives the poem its much-quoted fool / hool couple;
* hool = whole / hool is the Middle-English form that Chaucer sets as the rhyme partner of fool / cool in the lover’s self-mockery;
* countour, the ME form of ‘contour / counter’, is likewise used line-finally and is rhymed with tour / dour / colour in the description of the palace / hunt in the dream.

Hence Chaucer does make a rhyme with wente, here, fool, hool and countour in the Book of the Duchess; there is no word among A–E that he leaves unrhymed.

